  "full_response": "A typical Kubernetes cluster comprises various components that are deployed across multiple machines, organized into the Control Plane (Master) and the Data Plane (Nodes). Here's a breakdown of each component within these two planes, along with their roles:\n\n### Control Plane (Master Components)\n\n1. **kube-apiserver**:\n   - The central management entity that exposes the Kubernetes API.\n   - It is the entry point for all cluster operations.\n   - It handles REST requests, validates them, and updates a dynamic Etcd datastore with all objects (pods, services, etc).\n\n2. **etcd**:\n   - A distributed key-value store that holds the configuration data of the cluster.\n   - It stores all cluster data, ensuring that APIs from the kube-apiserver can read a centralized consistent data source.\n\n3. **kube-scheduler**:\n   - Watches for newly created Pods with no assigned node, and selects a node for them to run on.\n   - Selects nodes based on resource availability, node selectors, affinity and anti-affinity specifications, data locality, inter-workload interference, and other constraints.\n\n4. **kube-controller-manager**:\n   - Runs the core control loops that are periodically triggered to regulate the state of the cluster.\n   - Composed of several smaller controllers, including the Node Controller, Replication Controller, Endpoint Controller, Service Account & Token Controllers.\n   - Ensures the desired state of the cluster matches the actual state.\n\n5. **cloud-controller-manager** (in cloud environments):\n   - Integrates cloud provider-specific logic.\n   - Manages controllers that interact with cloud providers: Node Controller, Route Controller, and Service Controller.\n\n### Data Plane (Node Components)\n\n1. **kubelet**:\n   - Resides on each node in the cluster to ensure the containers are running as expected.\n   - Periodically communicates with the kube-apiserver to retrieve the PodSpecs and manages files for containers using container runtimes like Docker or containerd.\n   - Reports back to the control plane about the node status via the `node status` endpoint and can evict Pods when resources become over-constrained.\n\n2. **kube-proxy**:\n   - Maintains network rules on nodes, allowing network communication to Pods from sources within or outside the cluster.\n   - Implements part of the Kubernetes Service concept by mapping incoming requests to service endpoints.\n\n3. **Container Runtime**:\n   - The software responsible for running containers (like Docker, containerd, or CRI-O).\n   - Communicates with kubelet to",
